{"meta": {"chapter_title": "Refined Prototype and Capstone Defense", "title": "Toolchain Disclosure and AI-USE.md", "description": "AIL 606 textbook section connecting toolchain disclosure and ai-use.md to prototype README traceability matrix connecting theory to screen changes, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the prototype README traceability matrix connecting theory to screen changes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next."], "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_refined_prototype_and_capstone_defense", "ai_use_disclosure", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 2, "top_tier_hardened": false, "course": "ail606-supplement", "chapter": "06", "section": "02", "prereq_section_ids": ["ail606-supplement/06/01"], "practice_ids": ["ail606_supplement_6_2_toolchain_disclosure_and_ai_use_md_artifact_signal", "ail606_supplement_6_2_toolchain_disclosure_and_ai_use_md_annotation_signal", "ail606_supplement_6_2_toolchain_disclosure_and_ai_use_md_ai_boundary", "ail606_supplement_6_2_toolchain_disclosure_and_ai_use_md_calibration"]}, "content": "# Toolchain Disclosure and AI-USE.md\n\n![Toolchain Disclosure and AI-USE.md textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/06-02-toolchain-disclosure-and-ai-use-md.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA blanket statement such as \"AI tools were used throughout this project\" is technically true and academically useless. It satisfies a disclosure box without letting anyone evaluate what the human actually contributed. Toolchain disclosure exists to solve a specific authorship problem: when generative tools can produce objectives, feedback text, images, and even rationale, a committee needs to know which decisions are yours. The AI-USE.md file is the instrument that answers this, and it answers it at the granularity of individual contributions, not at the granularity of the whole project.\n\nThe deeper purpose is calibrating credit and responsibility. If an AI drafted your assessment items and you kept them unchanged, you are responsible for their alignment to your objectives whether or not you wrote them. If you rejected most of an AI's output, that rejection is itself evidence of expertise and belongs in the record. Disclosure is therefore not a compliance afterthought; it is part of the argument that the design judgment in the capstone is your own.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the prototype README traceability matrix connecting theory to screen changes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA reviewer opens two capstones. The first contains the sentence \"ChatGPT, Claude, and Midjourney were used to assist this project.\" The reviewer learns nothing about authorship and has to treat the entire artifact as potentially AI-authored. The second contains a table: ChatGPT drafted Module 3 objectives (kept two, rejected three), Claude drafted quiz feedback (rewrote four of six because the explanations contradicted the key), Midjourney produced a decorative banner (used as-is, flagged as non-instructional). The reviewer can now see exactly where human judgment overrode the tool, and the second capstone reads as more rigorous precisely because it discloses more, not less.\n\nThis inversion is the point. Specific, located disclosure builds trust; vague disclosure spends it.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nAn AI-USE.md is a **per-contribution provenance record**. Its unit of analysis is the discrete output, not the tool and not the project. Each row makes three things auditable: where the tool acted in the artifact, what it produced, and what human judgment followed. The third column is where authorship is established, because the same tool output can represent very different levels of human contribution.\n\nA useful frame is the gradient of human involvement, adapted from authorship and attribution norms:\n\n| Disposition | What it means | What it signals to a committee |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Generated and kept as-is | Tool output used unchanged | Lowest human contribution; acceptable for low-stakes or decorative content if flagged |\n| Generated and edited | Tool output revised by the designer | Human curation; show what changed and why |\n| Generated and rejected | Tool output considered and declined | Highest evidence of expertise; the rejection is the contribution |\n| Tool-assisted process | Tool used to brainstorm or critique, not to produce final text | Discloses influence on thinking, not on the artifact surface |\n\nMatch each disposition to the signal it sends a committee. The same tool output can sit at very different points on this gradient depending on what you did next.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Generated and kept as-is\",\"definition\":\"Lowest human contribution; acceptable only for low-stakes or decorative content if flagged\"},{\"term\":\"Generated and edited\",\"definition\":\"Human curation; you must show what changed and why\"},{\"term\":\"Generated and rejected\",\"definition\":\"Highest evidence of expertise; the rejection itself is the contribution\"},{\"term\":\"Tool-assisted process\",\"definition\":\"Discloses influence on your thinking, not on the artifact surface\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\nTwo norms govern the record. First, locate every contribution: \"used throughout\" is a non-disclosure because it cannot be checked against any specific element. Second, disclose rejections, not just acceptances; an entry showing you discarded three of five AI-drafted objectives is stronger evidence of instructional expertise than an entry showing you wrote them alone, because it demonstrates you can recognize a misaligned objective when a fluent tool produces one. The danger the file guards against is automation bias: accepting plausible-sounding output (a quiz explanation that contradicts the answer key) because it reads smoothly. The disclosure of an override is the trace that you resisted that bias.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nHere is the same AI-USE.md disclosure written two ways. Read the before version, then the annotated after version.\n\n**Before (weak disclosure):**\n\n> AI tools (ChatGPT, Claude, Midjourney) were used throughout this capstone to support design and writing.\n\nA reviewer cannot locate a single contribution in that sentence. Now the same project disclosed as an auditable table:\n\n**After (annotated disclosure entries):**\n\n| Tool | Where it acted | What I did with the output |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ChatGPT | Draft of Module 3 learning objectives | Kept 2 of 5; rejected 3 because they targeted recall, not the analyze-level goal in my rubric |\n| Claude | First pass of Module 2 quiz feedback text | Rewrote 4 of 6 items; the AI explanations contradicted the answer key |\n| Midjourney | Banner image for the welcome screen | Used as-is; logged the prompt and flagged it as decorative, not instructional |\n\nNotice the three columns: tool, location, and the human judgment that followed. The decorative-image row is honest about a low-stakes use. The two text rows show where I overrode the tool and why. A defense committee can now audit each AI contribution to a specific place in the work, which is exactly what a blanket \"used throughout\" statement hides.\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBefore you finalize the AI-USE.md, audit it for locatability and for honest rejections. A record that lists only tools and only accepted outputs is hiding the two things a reviewer most wants to see.\n\n1. **Locatability:** Can a reviewer point to the exact screen, item, or asset each entry refers to? If an entry says \"throughout,\" split it.\n2. **Disposition clarity:** Does each entry state kept, edited, or rejected, with the reasoning for edits and rejections?\n3. **Responsibility match:** For anything kept as-is, are you prepared to defend it as if you authored it, since in attribution terms you now own it?\n\nIf you cannot answer all three, the disclosure is not yet defensible, even if every tool is named.\n\n<inline-check question=\"Your AI-USE.md row reads: \\\"Claude: used throughout for quiz feedback; kept the good ones.\\\" Which decision-rule test does this row fail first?\" explanation=\"\\\"Throughout\\\" cannot be pointed at a specific item, so it fails locatability. Splitting it into per-item rows (which items, kept vs rewritten, and why) is the fix, before you even get to disposition clarity.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Locatability, because \\\"throughout\\\" names no specific item a reviewer could check\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Responsibility match, because you cannot defend kept output\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"None, because the tool and disposition are both named\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Disposition clarity only, because \\\"good ones\\\" is vague\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most damaging misreading is that more disclosure looks worse. Designers sometimes minimize AI mentions to seem more independent, which backfires: an under-disclosed capstone that a committee later suspects of AI authorship is far weaker than one that openly documents and bounds its tool use. Transparency about a rejection is a credential, not a confession.\n\nA second misreading is treating disclosure as separate from the prototype README traceability matrix connecting theory to screen changes. The two documents should agree.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"The under-disclosure temptation\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"An AI drafted your Module 3 objectives. You rejected 3 of 5 because they targeted recall, not your analyze-level goal. The deadline is close and you worry that disclosing AI use will make the capstone look less independent. What do you do?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Omit the AI mention entirely so the work reads as fully yours\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"Under-disclosure is the high-risk move. If a committee later suspects AI authorship, an undocumented capstone is far weaker than a documented one.\"},{\"label\":\"Log a vague \\\"AI tools were used throughout\\\" line to cover yourself\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"This satisfies a box but is non-disclosure: nothing is locatable, so the reviewer must treat the whole artifact as potentially AI-authored.\"},{\"label\":\"Log the rejection explicitly: 3 of 5 objectives declined, with the alignment reason\",\"next\":\"n4\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. The rejection is your strongest credential because it shows you can catch a misaligned objective a fluent tool produced.\"}]},\"n2\":{\"prompt\":\"You omitted it. In the defense a committee member notes the objectives read like generated text and asks who wrote them. What does the omission cost you now?\",\"feedback\":\"You now look like you hid AI use rather than reasoned about it. The rejection that was your best evidence of expertise is invisible, and trust is spent.\",\"choices\":[]},\"n3\":{\"prompt\":\"The vague line is in the file. A reviewer asks which specific objectives the tool produced and which you kept. Can the line answer?\",\"feedback\":\"No. \\\"Throughout\\\" cannot be checked against any element, so the reviewer cannot locate your authorship boundary and the disclosure does no work.\",\"choices\":[]},\"n4\":{\"prompt\":\"The explicit rejection is logged. How does the committee read it?\",\"feedback\":\"As evidence of instructional judgment: you recognized recall-level objectives masquerading as analyze-level ones and overrode the tool. Disclosing more made the capstone read as more rigorous, not less.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n If your matrix attributes a screen's design to a named principle but the AI-USE.md shows that screen's text was generated and kept unchanged, the committee will want to know who actually applied the principle. Keep the provenance record and the rationale record consistent.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nBuild the file by walking your own production history, not your final artifact. Open each component (objectives, assessments, feedback, media, narration) and ask one question per component: did a tool touch this, and if so, what did I do next? Write the answer as a row even when the answer is \"no tool.\" The act of asking it for every component is what prevents the silent, undocumented use that erodes trust.\n\nThen test one row against the responsibility standard. Take a row marked \"kept as-is\" and imagine defending that content's quality to a committee. If you can, the row is fine. If you cannot, that is a signal you outsourced a decision you have not yet evaluated, and the right move is to evaluate it now, edit if needed, and update the disposition. The walkthrough turns disclosure from a clerical list into a final quality pass.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, map every entry to a place where you can defend or revise the human contribution. The test is adversarial: assume the committee suspects every fluent sentence was AI-generated, and check whether your file gives them the evidence to rule that out where it is false and confirm it where it is true.\n\nA strong reader of toolchain disclosure can do four things:\n\n1. Point any disclosure entry to a specific element in the artifact.\n2. State the disposition (kept, edited, rejected) and the reason for each non-trivial entry.\n3. Defend any kept-as-is output as if they authored it.\n4. Show that the disclosure and the design rationale tell the same story.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong AI-USE.md is judged by whether a reviewer could reconstruct your authorship boundary from it alone. That boundary is the line between what you decided and what a tool produced. The standard fails when the file is a list of tool names with no locations or dispositions, because such a list moves the authorship question to the reviewer instead of answering it. When an entry's disposition is unclear, annotate the underlying element and request a comparison of a defensible versus an under-disclosed entry rather than a generic explanation of citation.\n\nA final paragraph for the artifact would read like this: \"I disclosed each AI contribution to a specific element and stated whether I kept, edited, or rejected it. I am prepared to defend every kept-as-is output as my own, and the rejections I logged are part of my evidence of design judgment.\"\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ai_use_disclosure\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\", \"ail606_supplement_refined_prototype_and_capstone_defense\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the disclosure requirement into a workplace with a strict client confidentiality policy that forbids naming external AI services in deliverables. The provenance principle survives, but the format adapts: instead of a public AI-USE.md, you maintain an internal provenance log and disclose at the level of process (\"portions of draft copy were machine-generated and human-reviewed against the alignment rubric\") in the deliverable itself. The test of understanding is whether you can preserve locatability and disposition under a constraint that limits how openly you can name the tool, rather than abandoning provenance because the public format is unavailable.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the prototype README traceability matrix connecting theory to screen changes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/06-02-toolchain-disclosure-and-ai-use-md-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"prototype README traceability matrix connecting theory to screen changes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"06.02\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **Mayer multimedia learning**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the prototype README traceability matrix connecting theory to screen changes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the prototype README traceability matrix connecting theory to screen changes for AIL 606 06.02. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to cognitive load theory. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_toolchain_disclosure_and_ai_use_md\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm that every AI-USE.md entry names a location, states a disposition, and that the file agrees with your traceability matrix. Locatable, honest, consistent disclosure is what turns tool use from a liability into evidence of judgment.\n\n## References\n\n- Mayer multimedia learning. https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_6_2_toolchain_disclosure_and_ai_use_md_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_ai_use_disclosure", "stem": "A student's AI-USE.md reads in full: 'Tools used: ChatGPT, Claude, Midjourney.' What is the core problem with this disclosure for a capstone defense?", "options": ["It names too many tools and looks excessive.", "It is a tool inventory, not a disclosure: it never says where each tool acted or what the designer did with the output.", "It should alphabetize the tool names.", "Midjourney is not an approved tool."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A defensible AI-USE.md maps each tool to a location in the artifact and to the designer's accept, edit, or reject decision.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_6_2_disclosure_as_tool_list", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["ai_use_disclosure"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_6_2_toolchain_disclosure_and_ai_use_md_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_ai_use_disclosure", "stem": "Which AI-USE.md entry lets a reviewer actually locate and audit the AI contribution?", "options": ["AI was used throughout the project to improve quality.", "I leaned on AI heavily during the design phase.", "Claude drafted the first version of the Module 2 quiz feedback text; I rewrote 4 of 6 items because the explanations contradicted the rubric.", "Various generative tools supported the work as needed."], "correct_index": 2, "explanation": "A locatable entry names the tool, the exact artifact location, and the human judgment that followed; the others are blanket overclaims.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_6_2_blanket_disclosure_overclaim", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ai_use_disclosure"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_6_2_toolchain_disclosure_and_ai_use_md_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_ai_supported_learning", "stem": "An entry says: 'ChatGPT generated the learning objectives for Module 3.' A reviewer asks what is missing. What should you add?", "options": ["The date and time the prompt was sent.", "What you verified, edited, or rejected after the tool produced the objectives, so authorship of the decision is clear.", "The exact prompt wording, character for character.", "A note that ChatGPT is widely used in industry."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Disclosure must show the human judgment that followed the AI output; otherwise the decision's authorship is undocumented.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_6_2_disclosure_omits_human_judgment", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_6_2_toolchain_disclosure_and_ai_use_md_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_evidence_based_revision", "stem": "A peer writes their entire AI-USE.md the night before defense and finds several entries are guesses about what the tool did weeks earlier. Which practice would have prevented this?", "options": ["Using a longer file with more headings.", "Asking an AI to reconstruct the history from the final files.", "Updating AI-USE.md as a running log at the moment each tool is used, with a one-line note on the location and decision.", "Disclosing only the tools used in the last week to stay accurate."], "correct_index": 2, "explanation": "A disclosure logged in real time is accurate and auditable; reconstruction from memory produces the vague entries the reviewer flags.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_6_2_disclosure_as_after_the_fact", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["evidence_based_revision"]}]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_6_2_disclosure_as_tool_list", "pattern": "disclosure_as_tool_list", "description": "Treating AI-USE.md as a flat list of tool names rather than a record of where each tool acted and what the designer did with the output", "trigger": "AI-USE.md says 'Used ChatGPT and Midjourney' with no location or decision recorded", "feedback": "A disclosure entry needs three parts: which tool, where it touched the artifact, and what you accepted, edited, or rejected.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_6_2_blanket_disclosure_overclaim", "pattern": "blanket_disclosure_overclaim", "description": "Writing a single blanket statement ('AI was used throughout') that overclaims and prevents a reviewer from locating any specific contribution", "trigger": "disclosure cannot point to a specific section, asset, or line the tool produced", "feedback": "Replace the blanket statement with per-artifact entries so a reviewer can trace each AI contribution to a place in the work.", "rail_action": "ask"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_6_2_disclosure_omits_human_judgment", "pattern": "disclosure_omits_human_judgment", "description": "Disclosing that a tool generated content but omitting what the designer verified, corrected, or rejected, so authorship of the decision is unclear", "trigger": "entry records the AI output but never the designer's review action", "feedback": "For each AI contribution, log the human judgment that followed: verified against a source, edited for accuracy, or rejected and replaced.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_6_2_disclosure_as_after_the_fact", "pattern": "disclosure_as_after_the_fact", "description": "Reconstructing AI-USE.md from memory at submission time rather than logging tool use as it happens, producing vague or missing entries", "trigger": "learner writes the whole disclosure the night before defense with no per-session notes", "feedback": "Treat AI-USE.md as a running log updated when the tool is used, not a memory exercise at the end.", "rail_action": "practice"}]}